We first recall the idea behind the Moore’s original reduction to Cat state [23, 27]. For any nekomata \ket{
u_{n}}_{T,A} with n targets T and ancillae A, we have the following property. Let L be a layer of n \mathtt{CZ} gates each acting on an input qubit and a corresponding target from T. On any input { m{x}}, the state L\ket{
u_{n}}_{T,A}\ket{{ m{x}}} is orthogonal to \ket{
u_{n}}_{T,A} iff { m{x}} has odd parity. To extract this information, we apply the reflection R=(I-2\outerproduct*{
u_n}{
u_n}_{T,A}\otimes\outerproduct*{+}{+}_{q}) on a fresh output register q, which acts as a controlled-X_{q} operator, controlled on T,A being \ket{
u_{n}}. R can be implemented using the circuit C for \ket{
u_{n}}, though,

We use contractions such as  ra{
u}_{T} on states acting on larger sub-systems, \ket{\psi}_{T,A}. The notation  ra{
u}_{T}\cdot\ket{\psi}_{T,A} refers to the un-normalized vector obtained by tracing out the T sub-system in \outerproduct*{
u}{
u}_{T}\otimes I_{A}\cdot\ket{\psi}. We also use the shorthand  ra{\psi}\cdot M\cdot\ket{\psi} for the operator given by ( ra{\psi}\otimes I)M(\ket{\psi}\otimes I) when it is clear that the matrix M acts on space larger than \ket{\psi}. Then, Definition 3.1 can be rewritten, f_{C}({ m{x}}):= raket{{ m{x}},0^{m}|C^{\dagger}Z_{t}C|{ m{x}},0^{m}}
